## Overview {#sec-overview}

The purpose of my project is to compare compare Gestational Diabetes (GDM) pregnancy outcomes by race and insurance status at Beth Israel Hospital between 2008 and 2022. A study on GDM prevalence by race has not been conducted in Boston, where Beth Israel is located. There have also been few studies on the affect of insurance status on GDM prevalence. The goal of my project is to contribute to the existing literature on the affect of race and insurance status on GDM prevalence.

## Introduction {#sec-introduction}

About 9% of pregnant women are diagnosed with Gestational Diabetes (GDM) in the United States Annually (American Diabetes Association). Poorly controlled diabetes in pregnancy poses several risks to mothers and their babies. These adverse outcomes include fetal macrosomia, neonatal hypoglycemia, respiratory distress syndrome, gestational hypertension, preeclampsia, cesarean section, preterm birth, and long-term increased risk of metabolic disease for both mothers and babies (Malaza et al., 2022). Several studies have found that Black, Hispanic, and Asian women are more likely to be diagnosed with GDM than white women (DeSisto et al., 2014; Erbetta et al., 2022). Many of these studies, however, were conduced using data from 1995-2012. The goal of this project is to look at GDM diagnosis prevalence at Beth Israel Hospital in Boston Massachusetts using data from MIMIC-IV.

This study will combine clinical knowledge of diabetes in pregnancy with biomedical informatics techniques to clean data and run several regression analyses. It requires an understanding of ICD procedure and diagnosis coding, data refinement, and statistical analyses.

## Methods {#sec-methods}

To complete the analysis on GDM diagnosis prevalence and pregnancy outcomes by race and insurance, the MIMIC-IV data set will be used. MIMIC-IV contains demographics, diagnoses, procedures, prescription, and lab data for patients admitted to Beth Israel Hospital from 2008 to 2022.

