#### Logistic Regressions

I ran several logistic regressions to test the significance of the differences in GDM prevalence among race, ethnicity, and insurance type. Models 1 and 3 function as base models, that is they examine only the relationship between odds of a GDM diagnosis and race/ethnicity and insurance type respectively. In Models 2 and 4, I expand upon the base models by controlling for maternal age. In Model 5, I combine the above models to examine the effects of race/ethnicity and insurance type while controlling for age. Based on feedback I received in class, I split the data into two subsets, one with only publicly insured patients and the other with only privately insured patients. I then ran the Model 2 regression on these data sets to examine the effects of race/ethnicity on the odds of GDM diagnosis controlling for maternal age in both populations independently.

```{r}


#base regression looking at the effects of race/ethnicity on odds of having a GDM diagnosis
Model_1 <- glm(has_GDM ~ race_collapsed,
    data = pregnancy_summary, family = binomial)

#effects of race/ethnicity on odds of having a GDM diagnosis controlling for age
Model_2 <- glm(has_GDM ~ race_collapsed + anchor_age,
          data = pregnancy_summary,
          family = binomial)

#base regression looking at the effects of insurance type on odds of having a GDM diagnosis
Model_3 <- glm(has_GDM ~ insurance,
          data = pregnancy_summary,
          family = binomial)

#effects of insurance type on odds of having a GDM diagnosis controlling for age
Model_4 <- glm(has_GDM ~ insurance + anchor_age,
          data = pregnancy_summary,
          family = binomial)

#effects of race/ethnicity on odds of having a GDM diagnosis controlling for insurance type and age
Model_5 <- glm(has_GDM ~ race_collapsed + insurance + anchor_age,
          data = pregnancy_summary,
          family = binomial)

#data set containing only patients on public insurance (Medicaid)
public_summary <- pregnancy_summary %>%
  filter(insurance %in% c("Medicaid"))

#regression for effects of race/ethnicity on odds of GDM diagnosis controlling for age for publically insured patients
Model_6 <- glm(has_GDM ~ race_collapsed + anchor_age,
               data = public_summary,
               family = binomial)

#data set containing only patients on private insurance
private_summary <- pregnancy_summary %>%
  filter(insurance %in% c("Private"))

#regression for effects of race/ethnicity on odds of GDM diagnosis controlling for age for privately insured patients
Model_7 <- glm(has_GDM ~ race_collapsed + anchor_age,
               data = private_summary,
               family = binomial)



models <- list(Model_1, Model_2, Model_3, Model_4, Model_5, Model_6, Model_7)

tidy(Model_1)

#Stargazer was giving incorrect astericks for p-values, so I pulled them out of each model and inputted them manually
extract_stargazer_inputs <- function(model) {
  s <- summary(model)$coefficients
  
  coefs  <- exp(s[, "Estimate"]) #exponentiating coefficents to give odds ratio
  ses    <- exp(s[, "Estimate"]) * s[, "Std. Error"]  
  pvals  <- s[, "Pr(>|z|)"]
  
  list(coefs = coefs, ses = ses, pvals = pvals)
}

inputs <- lapply(models, extract_stargazer_inputs)

coef_list <- lapply(inputs, function(x) x$coefs)
se_list   <- lapply(inputs, function(x) x$ses)
p_list    <- lapply(inputs, function(x) x$pvals)


#stargazer table
stargazer(
  models,
  coef = coef_list,
  se   = se_list,
  p    = p_list,
  type = "html",
  out = "regression_table.html",
  title = "Regression Results",
  dep.var.labels = "GDM Diagnosis (OR)",
  covariate.labels = c("Black",
                       "Hispanic",
                       "Asian",
                       "Other/NA",
                       "Age",
                       "Medicaid"),
  keep.stat = c("n"),
  star.cutoffs = c(0.05, 0.01, 0.001)
)


htmltools::includeHTML("regression_table.html")
```

Across patients who delivered at Beth Israel Hospital between 2008 and 2022, race and insurance type were both strongly associated with the odds of having a gestational diabetes (GDM) diagnosis. In the unadjusted race model (Model 1), Black, Hispanic, Asian, and Other/Unknown populations all had significantly higher odds of a GDM diagnosis compared to White patients. Asian patients showed the largest difference with an odds ratio of 2.723. After adjusting for age (Model 2), the odds ratios increased slightly across all populations compared to White patients.

Insurance type was also associated with GDM, but not as strongly as race and ethnicity. In the unadjusted model (Model 3), publicly insured patients had about 1.09 times the odds of GDM compared with privately insured patients, but this result was not significant at the 95% confidence level. This relationship persisted and became statistically significant after adjusting for age (Model 4), with an odds ratio of 1.369.The combined model (Model 5) showed that race/ethnicity and insurance remained independent predictors of a GDM diagnosis. Asian patients continued to have the highest risk, and public insurance remained associated with GDM, even after adjusting for race and age.

Stratified models showed that these racial disparities were present in both insurance groups. Among publicly insured patients (Model 6), Asian patients continued to have about twice the odds of developing GDM compared to White patients. Among privately insured patients (Model 7), the same racial pattern persisted, with Asian patients again showing the largest effect. It is important to note the reduced sample size in the stratified model due to splitting the population into two.

## Conclusion

Race and insurance status were both strong predictors of gestational diabetes for patients at Beth Israel. After adjusting for age, which is known to be associated with developing GDM, Asian, Hispanic, and Black patients had significantly higher odds of a GDM diagnosis compared to White patients. Public insurance, specifically Medicaid, was also independently associated with higher odds of GDM when adjusting for age. The racial disparities in GDM prevalence persisted within both publicly and privately insured populations, suggesting that differences in GDM prevalence are not explained solely by insurance type or age. Overall, the results point to real differences in GDM risk across demographic groups.

There are several limitations to this analysis. The data set is limited to patients who delivered at Beth Israel, which is an urban hospital in Boston, and the results cannot be applied on a national level. Additionally, because the data was pulled directly from EPIC with no validation on accuracy, some of the variables may be incorrect including ICD codes and race/ethnicity values. Finally, the data set is limited to lab and diagnosis values collected during inpatient hospital stays, so the regression cannot include controls for clinically relevant values such as hemoglobin A1c, BMI, and family history of diabetes. In the future, more robust analyses including national data, validated data, and outpatient data would be important for validating these initial results.

Again, this analysis demonstrates significant differences in GDM prevalence across patient populations at Beth Israel Hospital. These results point to future analyses of national GDM prevalence and on GDM delivery outcomes by race and insurance type.

